  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 151 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nThe main drivers of our local weather through the extended period \nwill be multiple areas of high pressure passing through the region. \nAt the start of the period on Tuesday, guidance consensus is that \nnorthwesterly flow aloft will keep Monday's post-frontal high \ngradually moving eastward. This in turn will have low-level flow\nout of the southwest for Tuesday and Wednesday due to the high \nlocated well east of the area. Despite continuing northwesterly \nflow aloft, the southwesterly low-level flow is ideal for a \nquick warm-up due to warm air advection and downsloping off of \nthe Ozarks. This translates to highs around 80 degrees on \nTuesday and into the mid-80s on Wednesday. \n\nThis warm-up will be stalled on Thursday, but to what degree and for \nhow long is uncertain as guidance diverges on the evolution of key \nfeatures into the weekend. Consensus is that another weak, \nuneventful cold front will move into the Middle Mississippi Valley \nWednesday night into Thursday and stall over or just south-southeast \nof the CWA. The center of the post-frontal airmass will quickly pass \nwell to the north of the CWA, providing only a minor decrease in \ntemperatures behind it on Thursday. This front will lift northward \nas a warm front thanks to upper-level ridging building into the \nregion and high pressure shifting eastward. While a majority of\nensemble members favor this solution, when it happens and to \nwhat degree remains uncertain. The spread between the 25th and \n75th percentile of ensemble guidance for temperatures hovers \naround 10 degrees Friday through the weekend. Despite the \nspread, confidence is high that temperatures will at the very \nleast be above normal thanks to the 25th percentile being in the\nupper 70s or warmer.",
